      "text": "To determine how many possible values of \\( k \\) make both roots of the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - 63x + k = 0 \\) prime numbers, we start by using Vieta's formulas. For a quadratic equation of the form \\( x^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), if the roots are \\( p \\) and \\( q \\), then:\n\n1. \\( p + q = -b \\)\n2. \\( pq = c \\)\n\nIn our equation \\( x^2 - 63x + k = 0 \\), we have \\( b = -63 \\) and \\( c = k \\). Therefore, we know that both roots are prime numbers, and their sum is 63 and their product is \\( k \\). Since \\( k \\) is the product of the roots, we need to find all pairs of prime numbers that sum to 63.\n\nWe will check pairs of prime numbers that add up to 63. We begin with the smallest prime number, 2, and continue with subsequent odd prime numbers (since the sum of an even and any odd number is odd, and 63 is odd).\n\n1. If \\( p = 2 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 2 = 61 \\). Both 2 and 61 are prime numbers, so \\( k = 2 \\times 61 = 122 \\).\n2. If \\( p = 3 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 3 = 60 \\). However, 60 is not a prime number, so we move on.\n3. If \\( p = 5 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 5 = 58 \\). Again, 58 is not a prime number.\n4. If \\( p = 7 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 7 = 56 \\). 56 is not a prime number.\n5. If \\( p = 11 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 11 = 52 \\). 52 is not a prime number.\n6. If \\( p = 13 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 13 = 50 \\). 50 is not a prime number.\n7. If \\( p = 17 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 17 = 46 \\). 46 is not a prime number.\n8. If \\( p = 19 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 19 = 44 \\). 44 is not a prime number.\n9. If \\( p = 23 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 23 = 40 \\). 40 is not a prime number.\n10. If \\( p = 29 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 29 = 34 \\). 34 is not a prime number.\n11. If \\( p = 31 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 31 = 32 \\). 32 is not a prime number.\n12. If \\( p = 37 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 37 = 26 \\). 26 is not a prime number.\n13. If \\( p = 41 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 41 = 22 \\). 22 is not a prime number.\n14. If \\( p = 43 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 43 = 20 \\). 20 is not a prime number.\n15. If \\( p = 47 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 47 = 16 \\). 16 is not a prime number.\n16. If \\( p = 49 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 49 = 14 \\). 14 is not a prime number.\n17. If \\( p = 53 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 53 = 10 \\). 10 is not a prime number.\n18. If \\( p = 59 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 59 = 4 \\). 4 is a prime number.\n19. If \\( p = 61 \\), then \\( q = 63 - 61 = 2 \\). Both 61 and 2 are prime numbers, so \\( k = 61 \\times 2 = 122 \\).\n\nThus, the only possible value for \\( k \\) is 122. Therefore, the number of possible values of \\( k \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
